def LAD(*a): return list(a)
def RUB(a,b,c,d): return [(a,'Missing or unclear','Meets the stated check',b),(c,'Guessing','Evidence kept',d),('Records','Gaps','Complete','Complete and reviewed by a peer'),('Safety and ethics','Overlooked','Followed','Followed and taught to others')]
COURSES = [
dict(slug='research-methods', title='Research Methods and Applied Inquiry', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Frame questions, collect and analyse evidence, and report findings clearly and ethically, so that decisions in a school, shop, clinic or small firm rest on data and not on guesses.',
 outs=[('Define','a research question that can be answered with data.'),('Apply','sampling and data collection methods.'),('Analyse','data with simple counts, averages and charts.'),('Evaluate','sources, bias and the limits of a study.'),('Create','a ten-page report with a clear recommendation.')],
 task='Complete a small applied study with at least 60 responses and write a ten-page report.', cond='About 10 weeks part-time. A real local topic, a computer with a spreadsheet, a phone for calls, a mentor.',
 standard='The question is answerable; the method and sample are described; consent is recorded; data is cleaned and analysed; limits are stated; every claim in the report links to a number.', who='Students, analysts, teachers, programme staff, shop and clinic managers.', req='Basic arithmetic and the ability to read a table.',
 mods=[
 M('Frame a question','Define',4,['Start from a decision someone must make, then ask what evidence would help.','Write the question so it names who, what and where, for example: how many households in ward 12 buy water cans each week.','Split a broad topic into two or three small questions.','Reject questions that need opinion words such as best or good unless you define them.'],'The broad topic better health becomes: What share of 40 shop workers in one market skip lunch on 3 or more days a week?','Rewrite three broad topics as answerable questions.','Each question names the group, the measure and the place.','What makes a research question answerable?','It names who, what and where, and can be answered with data you can collect.'),
 M('Review existing evidence','Analyse',4,['Search for official statistics, past surveys and local reports before collecting new data.','Note who wrote each source, when, and how they collected data.','Prefer recent sources with a stated method and sample size.','Keep a source table with title, year, finding and weakness.'],'Of six sources found, two are older than 2015 and one has no sample size; three go into the source table.','Build a source table of six sources for your question.','Each row has year, method, finding and one weakness.','Why read past evidence first?','It saves effort, shows what is already known and sharpens your question.'),
 M('Choose a design and sample','Apply',5,['Match the method to the question: counts need a survey, reasons need interviews, change needs a before and after.','Define the population and a sampling frame, such as a list of 400 customers.','Pick a random sample by drawing numbers, not the first people you meet.','Decide sample size: about 80 of 400 gives a rough but useful picture.'],'From a list of 400 customers, draw every fifth name after a random start between 1 and 5 to get 80 names.','Write a one-page design with a sample plan.','Population, sample size and selection method are stated.','Why prefer a random sample to asking whoever is nearby?','It reduces selection bias so the sample looks more like the population.'),
 M('Build the instrument and consent','Create',5,['Write each survey question with one idea and simple words.','Offer answer choices that do not overlap, such as 0, 1 to 2, 3 or more.','Pilot the form with five people and fix confusing items.','Prepare a consent note that explains purpose, voluntary taking part and how data is kept private.'],'In a pilot of 5 people, 3 misread Question 4 about last week; it is reworded to name the dates.','Produce a 10-question survey and a consent note.','Pilot changes are logged and consent wording is plain.','Why run a pilot?','To find confusing questions before they spoil the real data.'),
 M('Collect data carefully','Apply',5,['Record each answer on the day in a table with an ID, not a name.','Check forms for blanks while the respondent is still present.','Keep a log of refusals and unreachable people.','Store files in a locked folder and delete phone numbers after the study.'],'Of 80 names, 62 respond and 18 refuse or cannot be reached, a response rate of 62 divided by 80, about 78 percent.','Collect at least 60 responses and keep a response log.','Response rate is calculated and the log lists non-responses.','What is a response rate?','The share of chosen people who actually answered.'),
 M('Clean and analyse data','Analyse',6,['Remove duplicates, fix typing errors and mark missing values, keeping the raw file untouched.','Count each answer and turn counts into percentages.','Compute the average and the middle value; use the middle value when a few answers are very large.','Compare two groups with a simple table and a bar chart.'],'Daily spend of 9 customers: 40, 45, 50, 50, 55, 60, 60, 65, 400. The mean is 91.7 but the median is 55, so report the median.','Analyse your data and make two charts.','Raw file is kept, figures match a recount and charts have labels.','When is the median better than the mean?','When a few extreme values pull the mean away from the typical case.'),
 M('Judge bias and limits','Evaluate',4,['List sources of bias: who was missed, who refused, how questions led.','State what the study cannot show, for example that two things moving together does not prove one causes the other.','Give a margin: with 62 answers a 50 percent result could reasonably be 10 points either way.','Check the findings against past evidence.'],'60 percent of 62 say yes; the true share could plausibly be 48 to 72 percent, so avoid saying a majority for sure.','Write a half-page limits section.','At least three limits are named with their likely effect.','Why state limits in a report?','So readers know how far to trust the findings.'),
 M('Capstone: applied study report','Create',6,['Write the ten-page report: question, method, results, limits, recommendation.','Lead with a one-paragraph summary of the answer.','Use plain words, labelled charts and a source list.','Present for 10 minutes and answer questions.'],'A report finds 41 of 62 workers, 66 percent, skip lunch 3 or more days a week and recommends a 20-minute shaded break.','Submit the report and present it.','Every claim links to a number, limits are stated and the recommendation follows the data.','What proves a report is sound?','Claims match the data, the method can be repeated and limits are honest.')],
 extra=[('What is informed consent?','Taking part freely after understanding the purpose and use of the data.'),('Why keep the raw data file untouched?','So any cleaning can be checked and repeated.')],
 rubric=RUB('Question and method','Answerable question, random sample and tested instrument','Analysis','Correct figures, honest limits and clear charts'),
 ladder=LAD('Read simple tables and charts.','Explain a research question and a sample.','Run a small survey under guidance.','Design and analyse a study alone.','Judge bias and review the studies of others.','Lead research for a team or programme.','Set research standards and train others.'),
 jobs=['Research assistant','Data analyst','Survey coordinator','Programme monitoring officer','Market researcher']),
dict(slug='test-beds-for-product-development', title='Test Beds for New Product Development', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Plan, run and learn from test beds that prove a new product works safely before launch, so that a go or no-go decision rests on recorded results and not on hope.',
 outs=[('Define','test objectives with pass and fail limits.'),('Apply','a test plan on a repeatable rig.'),('Analyse','results with averages and failure counts.'),('Evaluate','risk and safety before launch.'),('Create','a test report with a go or no-go decision.')],
 task='Plan and run a small test of a new product, record results over at least 30 trials, and write a report with a go or no-go.', cond='About 10 weeks part-time. A working prototype, a safe test space, a stopwatch or meter, a spreadsheet, a mentor.',
 standard='Each test has a pass limit set before it starts; the rig is repeatable; at least 30 trials are logged; failures are explained; the decision follows the data.', who='Makers, engineers, founders, product trainees, quality staff in small units.', req='Basic arithmetic. A prototype or sample product.',
 mods=[
 M('Define the product claims','Define',4,['List what the product promises in numbers, for example lifts 10 kg or runs 6 hours.','Turn each promise into something you can measure.','Rank claims by risk to the user if they fail.','Write the conditions of use: heat, dust, voltage and user type.'],'A solar lamp promises 6 hours of light; the test claim is at least 6 hours at 80 percent of starting brightness after a full charge.','Write five measurable claims for your product.','Each claim has a number, a unit and a condition.','Why turn a promise into a number?','A number can be tested and a vague promise cannot.'),
 M('Set pass and fail limits','Define',4,['Choose limits before testing so results cannot bend them.','Use the rule that the product must meet the claim in at least 28 of 30 trials, which is about 93 percent.','Set a stop rule for dangerous failures: one smoke, spark or injury ends the test.','Get the limits agreed by a second person.'],'Limit: lamp brightness at hour 6 must be at least 80 percent in 28 of 30 trials; any overheating above 60 degrees Celsius stops the test.','Write pass, fail and stop limits for each claim.','Limits are written, dated and signed by a reviewer before the first trial.','Why set limits before testing?','So the result cannot be reinterpreted to look better.'),
 M('Design the test bed','Create',5,['Build a rig that holds conditions the same each trial: fixed load, fixed temperature, fixed method.','Choose instruments and check their accuracy, such as a meter that reads within 2 percent.','Mark a safe zone with guards and an emergency switch.','Plan how many units to test: at least 5 units, 6 trials each, gives 30 trials.'],'A bench with a fixed 5-watt load, a thermometer, a timer and a switch gives 5 lamps times 6 trials, 30 trials in all.','Sketch and build a rig, then run a dry trial.','The dry trial repeats within 5 percent.','What makes a test bed repeatable?','Fixed conditions, checked instruments and a written method.'),
 M('Write the test plan','Create',4,['List each test with objective, steps, tools, number of trials and limit.','Add a risk table with cause, effect and control.','Assign roles: runner, recorder and checker.','Set dates and a place for results.'],'Plan: 6 tests, 30 trials each for the main claim, 10 for side checks, 4 days at 3 hours a day.','Produce a plan of two to four pages.','Each test links to a claim and a limit.','What does a test plan list?','Objective, steps, tools, trials, limits, roles and risks.'),
 M('Run trials and record','Apply',6,['Follow the written steps exactly, with one change at a time.','Record every trial, including failures, in a table with date, unit, reading and note.','Never discard a result without a written reason.','Photograph set-up and key readings.'],'Of 30 trials, 27 pass, 2 fail on battery drop and 1 is voided because the meter lead was loose; the voided trial is repeated.','Run at least 30 trials and keep the log.','Log is complete, readable and every void has a reason.','Why record failures?','Failures show the weak points and protect honest decisions.'),
 M('Analyse results','Analyse',5,['Count passes and failures and show the pass rate.','Compute the average and the spread: lowest and highest.','Group failures by cause with a simple tally.','Find the likely cause of the top failure.'],'Pass rate 28 of 30 is 93 percent; 2 failures both trace to a loose connector, so fix the connector.','Make a results table and a failure tally.','Figures match a recount by a peer.','Why group failures by cause?','To fix the most common cause first.'),
 M('Evaluate risk and safety','Evaluate',5,['Rate each risk by how likely and how severe it is.','Check user safety: heat, shock, sharp edges and misuse.','Compare results with rules the product must meet in India, such as bureau of standards marks where they apply.','Decide what to fix, accept or block.'],'A connector fault has medium chance and high severity; it is fixed and 10 trials are repeated before any approval.','Complete a risk table for your product.','Every high risk has a control and a re-test.','Which risks must be fixed before launch?','High-severity risks to user safety.'),
 M('Capstone: test report and decision','Create',5,['Write the report: claims, limits, method, results, failures and risk.','State go, fix and retest, or no-go.','Give three next actions with owners and dates.','Present to a mentor panel.'],'Report: 28 of 30 pass, connector fixed, 10 re-tests pass, decision go for a pilot batch of 100 units.','Submit the report and present it.','Decision follows the limits and every figure is traceable to the log.','What proves a go decision?','Results meet the pre-set limits and risks are controlled.')],
 extra=[('Why change only one thing at a time?','So you know which change caused the result.'),('What is a stop rule?','A condition that ends a test at once to prevent harm.')],
 rubric=RUB('Test design','Pre-set limits, repeatable rig and clear plan','Analysis','Correct pass rates, failure causes and justified decision'),
 ladder=LAD('Know basic tools and measures.','Explain a claim and a pass limit.','Run a simple test under guidance.','Plan and run a test bed alone.','Review risk and certify results.','Lead product testing for a team.','Set test standards and train testers.'),
 jobs=['Test technician','Quality assurance assistant','Product tester','Prototype engineer','Pilot production coordinator']),
dict(slug='marketing-copywriting', title='Marketing Copywriting', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Write clear, honest copy for ads, pages, emails and posts that moves readers to act, and test each piece so that choices rest on results.',
 outs=[('Define','the audience, goal and one main message of a text.'),('Apply','headline, structure and call-to-action rules.'),('Analyse','a rival text and test results.'),('Evaluate','claims for truth and fairness.'),('Create','a portfolio of five tested pieces.')],
 task='Produce five pieces of marketing copy, test at least two of them, and assemble a short portfolio.', cond='About 8 weeks part-time. A computer, sample products or services of a local business, a free page or email tool, a mentor.',
 standard='Each piece has a stated goal and audience; claims are true and backed; copy is edited to a plain reading level; at least two pieces are tested with numbers.', who='Writers, marketers, students, small business owners.', req='Good command of the language you will write in.',
 mods=[
 M('Know the audience and goal','Define',4,['Describe one reader: who, what they need, what worries them.','Interview three real customers and note their exact words.','State one goal per text: click, call, buy or reply.','Write the main message in one sentence.'],'Goal: 30 messages from tailoring customers in a month. Message: Get a blouse stitched in 3 days, or the stitching is free.','Write a brief for a local product.','Brief states reader, goal and one-sentence message.','Why write one goal per text?','A text with many goals confuses the reader and weakens action.'),
 M('Study offers and rival copy','Analyse',4,['Collect five rival ads or pages.','Mark promise, proof and call to action in each.','Find what rivals all say and what none say.','Pick one honest difference for your product.'],'Four of five rivals say best quality; none state delivery time, so a 3-day promise stands out.','Analyse five rival texts in a table.','Table has promise, proof and gap for each.','What does a gap in rival copy give you?','A point of difference you can honestly claim.'),
 M('Write headlines and openings','Create',5,['Lead with the reader benefit and a number where true.','Write 10 headlines and pick the best 2.','Keep headlines under 12 words.','Make the first sentence earn the second.'],'Ten headlines drafted; the winner is Stitched in 3 days or free, at 9 words.','Write 10 headlines for one offer.','Top two are under 12 words and state a benefit.','Why write many headlines?','The first idea is rarely the best.'),
 M('Structure the body','Apply',5,['Use a simple order: problem, solution, proof, offer, action.','Write short sentences and short paragraphs.','Use words your reader uses, and cut jargon.','Check the reading level so a 14-year-old can follow it.'],'A 120-word page: 2 lines of problem, 3 of solution, 2 of proof, 1 offer, 1 button.','Write a 120-word page.','Structure is complete and the average sentence is under 16 words.','What order does a simple page follow?','Problem, solution, proof, offer, action.'),
 M('Write for each channel','Apply',5,['Ads: one idea and a clear button.','Email: a subject line under 45 characters and one link.','Social post: hook in the first line.','Messaging apps: short, polite and with an easy way to opt out.'],'An email subject, Your blouse is ready in 3 days, is 33 characters and has one link.','Write one ad, one email and one post.','Each fits its channel length and has one action.','Why give each message one action?','It makes the next step clear.'),
 M('Prove it and stay honest','Evaluate',4,['Back each claim with a fact, a number or a customer quote used with permission.','Avoid false urgency and made-up scarcity.','Follow the advertising code and consumer rules that apply in India.','Mark offers with dates and conditions.'],'The claim 3 days is backed by a log of 40 orders where 38 met the promise; the copy says 95 percent within 3 days.','Audit one text for claims and proof.','Each claim has a source and no claim is false.','Why check claims before publishing?','False claims harm readers and break advertising rules.'),
 M('Test and improve','Analyse',5,['Change one thing between versions A and B.','Send each version to at least 100 people at the same time.','Compare results: clicks divided by sends.','Keep the winner and note the lesson.'],'A gets 9 clicks of 100 and B gets 15 of 100; B wins at 15 percent, but retest with 200 as the sample is small.','Run one A and B test and record it.','Test changes one thing and results are shown as a rate.','Why change only one thing in a test?','Then you know what caused the difference.'),
 M('Capstone: copy portfolio','Create',5,['Produce five pieces: ad, page, email, post and message.','Add a goal, audience and result to each.','Edit twice with a peer.','Present the portfolio.'],'Portfolio of five pieces with two tests: email B lifted clicks from 9 to 15 percent.','Submit the portfolio.','Each piece shows goal, claim proof and edit history.','What proves copy works?','Test results against the stated goal.')],
 extra=[('What is a call to action?','The clear next step you ask the reader to take.'),('Why edit after writing?','To cut waste and fix unclear lines.')],
 rubric=RUB('Clarity','One message, simple words and clear action','Honesty and testing','True claims and tests recorded with numbers'),
 ladder=LAD('Read well and write clearly.','Explain audience, goal and message.','Write short copy under guidance.','Write and test copy alone.','Edit and review copy for others.','Lead a content team.','Set copy standards for a brand and train writers.'),
 jobs=['Copywriter','Content writer','Email marketing executive','Social media executive','Marketing assistant']),
]
